  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Sunitha R vs Vmm Fuel Station on 13 February, 2025 Kabc020344822023 Before The Chief Judge, Court Of Small Causes, Member Prl.Motor Accident Claims Tribunal At Bengaluru Dated This The 13th Day Of February, 2025",
    "facts": "Wife and mother of deceased Varadaraju K. filed petition under Section 166 of IMV Act claiming compensation worth of Rs.80,00,000/- from the respondents, who are the owner and insurer of the Tanker Lorry bearing No.KA-07-A-9958 alleging that on 07.10.2023 at 7.00 a.m. when Varadaraju riding Honda Activa bearing No.KA-41-EU-0560 from Channarayapattana towards Bengaluru, near Byaladakere gate on NH-75 within the limits of Amruthur police station, driver of Tanker Lorry bearing No.KA-07-A-9958 drove the same negligently from Yadiyur towards Bangalore, dashed the same to hind part of Honda Activa, thereby caused the accident, caused grievous injuries to Varadaraju. Immediately after accident, Varadaraju has been shifted to Govt. Hospital, Kunigal, wherein first aid has been provided, then shifted to Bengaluru, however, on the way of Bengaluru hospital, succumbed to the injuries. 2. As on the date of accident deceased Varadaraju being aged about 35 years, used to earn Rs.3,000/- per day by maintaining mobile street hotel and catering at Rajarajeswari Nagara, Bengaluru. 3. Regarding accident Amruthur police registered a case in crime No.153/2023 against the driver of the Tanker Lorry bearing No.KA-07-A-9958. 4. Inspite of service of notice, respondent No.1 owner of Lorry bearing No.KA-07-A-9958 remained absent. 5. In response to the notice respondent No.2, Insurance Company appeared through his Advocate filed written statement denying the allegation regarding reason and mode of accident, age, occupation and income of the deceased. During cross-examination of P.W.1, learned Advocate appearing for the Insurance Company get marked copy of Learner Licence of Varadaraju at Ex.R.1. On 23.10.2024 petitioners produced evidence of Nagaraju, S/o.Nanjundaiah as P.W.2. On 03.12.2024 Insurance Company produced the evidence of Officer as R.W.1 and produced 2 documents at Ex.R.2 and R.3. 8. 9. On the basis of material on record, my findings on the above issues are as under: Issue No.1 ... In the Affirmative; Issue No.2 ... Petitioners are entitle to receive Rs.28,09,120/- with current and future interest at 6%p..a. from the respondents as compensation. by analyzing the evidence available in the file requested to answer Issue No.1 in favour of the Insurance Company on the ground that as on the date of alleged accident, Varadaraju had Learner Licence to ride Honda Activa by relying on the following judgments: 1. AIR 1996 SC 1150 Civil Appeal No.3486/1986 (New India Assurance Co.Ltd. -Vs- Mandar Madhav Tambe and Others). 2. AIR 2011 SC 3547, SLP No.24020/2011 with 24021- 24023/2011 (Alka Ojha -Vs- Rajasthan Public Service Commission and Another). 3. ACJ 2005 1968, High Court of Delhi (Rama Nand Pandey and others -Vs- Nisha Tiwari and others).",
    "arguments": "11. In the lengthy argument, learned Advocate appearing for petitioners by analyzing the evidence available on file, requested to answer Issue No.1 in favour of petitioners on the ground that negligent driving of the Lorry bearing No.KA-07-A-9958 was the reason for the accident.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "11. In the lengthy argument, learned Advocate appearing for petitioners by analyzing the evidence available on file, requested to answer Issue No.1 in favour of petitioners on the ground that negligent driving of the Lorry bearing No.KA-07-A-9958 was the reason for the accident.",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
